A library system publishes returned‑book notifications to many independent display boards. The system keeps a list of registered listeners and calls them in a loop:

```java
public void publish(Notification n) {
    for (BoardListener listener : listeners) {
        listener.onNotify(n);
    }
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A configuration class in a library's returned‑books processing system is written like this and is read from every request handler:

```java
public class ReturnConfig {
    private static ReturnConfig instance;
    private ReturnConfig() { }
    public static ReturnConfig getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new ReturnConfig();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Which criticisms of this code are justified?

A library's overdue notice generator fixes the order of the steps and lets subclasses vary two of them:

```java
public abstract class NoticeGenerator {
    public final Notice generate() {
        Data data = load();
        Data shaped = transform(data);
        return write(render(shaped));
    }
    protected Data load() { return Data.fromDefaultSource(); }
    protected abstract Data transform(Data data);
    protected abstract String render(Data data);
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A library system sorts returned books with interchangeable algorithms:

```java
public interface SortStrategy { void performSort(List<Book> input); }
public class BubbleSort implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class MergeSort  implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class Context { private List<Book> books; private SortStrategy sortAlgorithm; ... }
public class Policy  { private Context context; public void configure() { ... } }
```

Using the class names from this listing: the class that holds the books and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `BubbleSort` and `MergeSort` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

A reviewer rejects the following class:

```java
public class OverdueNotifier {
    private final SmtpMailer mailer = new SmtpMailer("mail.internal", 25);
    public void sendNotification(User user) { /* ... */ mailer.send(user.email(), "..."); }
}
```

The reviewer's first objection is that `OverdueNotifier` names a concrete low‑level class instead of an abstraction such as a `Notifier` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer's fix is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two‑word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
